hings of  the world so con khazana me says you  know when you think about how you know  yourself a human being not only can know  these things out there but can't know  the things in themselves a human being  can't even know him or herself in what  they are entirely that's an interesting  idea isn't it he says  a person cannot pretend to know what  that person is in himself or in herself  from the knowledge he has or she has by  internal sensation inner sense as well  as external sense nor can we know what  we are entirely by the appearances of  our nature the characteristics that we  have but you know tendencies our  interests our inclinations our desires  all that stuff is us but it's of the  phenomenal world even how our  consciousness is affected by say other  human beings or by other things within  the phenomenal world is on the side of  the phenomenal world and insofar as  we're considering ourselves in that way  we're not really free we are actually  subject to you know laws of nature they  may not be the same laws of nature like  gravity or things like that they may be  much more complex it could be you know  psychosocial laws right or however we  want to think about it but there's going  to be laws that govern that on the other  side considered insofar as we are you  know a cause literally a primordial  thing orsa right cut we'll talk about  our what's translated as ego our ich in  German the the real me behind the  appearances insofar as I am that I am  actually free and insofar as I am that I  am like the thing in itself there is a  something that's me behind the  appearances of the me that you're seeing  right now on the me that I conceptualize  when I think of that you know the guy  that I happen to be in my memories and  and you know what I'm going to eat for  dinner any of those sorts of things that  is where the locus of human freedom lies  and Khan says that we can look at  ourselves in both of these ways  at the same time we can look at  ourselves 